"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the sub\u2011problem. For \\(n=3\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists. The attainable value of \\(k\\) is therefore \\(\\boxed{0}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. List all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le 4\\); there are six such points.  \n2. Recall that a line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n3. Choose three distinct non\u2011sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   L_{1}: y=1,\\qquad\n   L_{2}: x=1,\\qquad\n   L_{3}: y=2 .\n   \\]\n4. Verify that every required point lies on at least one of these lines, thereby covering the whole set with three non\u2011sunny lines and achieving \\(k=0\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the condition \u201cfor all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)\u201d becomes  \n\\[\na+b\\le 4,\\qquad a\\ge 1,\\; b\\ge 1 .\n\\]\nHence the set of points that must be covered is  \n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions:\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad \n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad \n\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\nThus a *non\u2011sunny* line must be of one of the three types above.\n\nConsider the three distinct lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\; y=1 &&\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\; x=1 &&\\text{(vertical)},\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\; y=2 &&\\text{(horizontal)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three are parallel to either the \\(x\\)-axis or the \\(y\\)-axis, so none of them is sunny; consequently the configuration contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe now check that every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of these lines:\n\n* \\(L_{1}\\) contains the points \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) (all points with \\(b=1\\)).\n* \\(L_{2}\\) contains the points \\((1,2),(1,3)\\) (all points with \\(a=1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\)).\n* \\(L_{3}\\) contains the point \\((2,2)\\) (the only remaining point).\n\nThus each of the six required points belongs to at least one of the three lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).\n\nSince the three lines are distinct and none is sunny, we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n=3\\) lines with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines satisfying the covering condition. \u220e"
